  "text_summary": "<think>Let me analyze this UART RX bug report carefully.\n\n**Fault Information:**\n- Module: uart_rx\n- Error type: 边界回绕 (boundary wrap-around)\n- Fail stage: bmc_depth_11, fail_step: 11\n- Trigger condition: state == S_IDLE\n- Failed line: 141\n- Fault cone includes: DATA, HALF, baud_cnt, baud_cnt_d, bit_cnt, clk, rst_n, rx_busy, rx_data, rx_valid, rxd, rxd_d, state, state_d\n\n**State Trajectory Analysis:**\n- Cycle 3: state=01 (S_IDLE if 00 is IDLE, or maybe 01 is START?), rx_busy=1, baud_cnt=0000\n- Cycles 3-11: state stays at \"01\", baud_cnt increments from 0000 to 1000 (8 counts)\n- Cycle 12: state transitions to \"10\", baud_cnt wraps back to 0000\n\n**Key observations:**\n1. State is stuck at \"01\" from cycle 3 through cycle 11, then transitions to \"10\" at cycle 12\n2. baud_cnt counts up from 0 to 8 (1000 in binary) over cycles 3-11\n3. At cycle 12, baud_cnt wraps from 1000 back to 0000 - this looks like the boundary wrap-around issue\n4. rx_busy stays at 1 throughout, rx_valid stays at 0\n5. bit_cnt remains at 0000 throughout - never increments\n\n**Analysis of the bug:**\n- The state \"01\" is likely S_START or some active state (since rx_busy=1)\n- At cycle 12, the state transitions to \"10\" - this might be unexpected\n- The trigger condition says \"state == S_IDLE\" was violated - meaning the assertion expected state to be S_IDLE but it wasn't\n- baud_cnt wrapped from 1000 (8) back to 0000 - this is the wrap-around behavior\n- bit_cnt never increments, suggesting we're not actually progressing through bit reception\n\nWait, let me reconsider. If state==S_IDLE is the failed assertion (meaning the assertion `state == S_IDLE` failed, i.e., state was NOT S_IDLE when it should have been), then:\n- At cycle 12, state went from \"01\" to \"10\" instead of returning to IDLE (which might be \"00\")\n- Or actually, looking at cycle 3: state was \"01\" initially\n\nActually, in many UART designs:\n- 00 = S_IDLE\n- 01 = S_START (detecting start bit)\n- 10 = S_DATA (receiving data)\n- 11 = S_STOP\n\nThe trigger_condition says \"state == S_IDLE\" was the failure condition. Looking at the state trajectory:\n- The state was in \"01\" for many cycles (presumably S_START) without transitioning to S_DATA\n- When baud_cnt wrapped from 8 to 0 at cycle 12, state transitioned to \"10\" (S_DATA) instead of going back to S_IDLE\n\nThe bug seems to be: After the start bit half-bit detection, when baud_cnt reaches its boundary, the state should transition properly, but instead it wraps incorrectly.\n\nActually, looking more carefully:\n- The error_type is \"边界回绕\" (boundary wrap-around)\n- baud_cnt is 4 bits (0000 to 1111, max 15)\n- In this trace, baud_cnt only goes from 0 to 8 (1000), which is within bounds\n- But the wrap happens at cycle 12 from 1000 back to 0000\n\nHmm, if HALF baud detection is involved, then the baud rate counter might be expected to count to HALF (typically 8 for 16x oversampling) before transitioning.\n\nLooking at this:\n- At cycle 12, baud_cnt goes from 1000 (8) back to 0000, and state transitions to \"10\"\n- This suggests the baud counter hit HALF (8) and should have transitioned state\n- But the issue is bit_cnt stayed at 0000 the entire time\n\nThe likely bug:\n- In uart_rx, when baud_cnt reaches HALF (the half-bit boundary), it should trigger state transition and reset bit_cnt or increment\n- The wrap-around at cycle 12 caused state to go to \"10\" but without proper bit_cnt update\n- The assertion expects state == S_IDLE at some point (perhaps after a complete byte reception or timeout), but state went to \"10\" instead\n\nMost suspicious code location: uart_rx module, around line 141, where the baud_cnt comparison with HALF/DATA boundary is performed, likely in the state transition logic for S_START or similar state.\n\nLet me write 3-5 sentences in Chinese describing this:\n\nThe defect occurs at cycle 12 when baud_cnt wraps from 1000 (8) back to 0000, causing an unexpected state t